body:

Time series foundation models promise forecasting without training: give the model your history and get a forecast back. But how well do they work in practice, and what does it take to use them well?

In this hands-on masterclass we use sktime, a unified, open-source Python framework for time series analysis and machine learning to evaluate and compare state-of-the-art foundation models with open weights side by side: Chronos-2 (Amazon), TimesFM 2.5 (Google), and t0-gamma (The Forecasting Company). The selection may change as new models become available before the conference.

We start with how these models work and what sets them apart. Using a public dataset, we define a business question and choose suitable data preparation and evaluation metrics. Rather than writing custom wrapper code for each model from scratch, we leverage sktime's standardized, scikit-learn-compatible interface to wrap all models and run a rolling-origin backtest, using simple baselines for comparison.
We then explore what can still be tuned when there is no training: context length, model size, inference settings and covariates.

The experiments from all participants feed into one shared leaderboard, with every model and setting evaluated on the same data with the same metrics. By the end of the day, we have an evidence-based picture of what works, what does not, and which choices make the biggest difference.

You are also welcome to bring your own data and see how the models perform on it.

# Who Should Attend

Data scientists, analysts, ML engineers and enthusiasts who work with Python and want to find out whether time series foundation models are worth trying on their own forecasting problems.

You do not need any experience with foundation models or transformers. Basic forecasting knowledge is helpful but not required; we recap the essentials.

# What You'll Learn

After the masterclass you will leave with:

- A clear understanding of how time series foundation models work, their capabilities and limitations.
- A practical workflow for designing reliable forecasting experiments, from defining a business question and choosing appropriate metrics to running backtests and establishing baselines.
- The ability to evaluate forecasts beyond accuracy, considering prediction stability and computational costs.
- A ready-to-use repository built on sktime containing notebooks, a unified forecasting interface, and backtesting code that can be adapted to your own datasets.

# What to Bring

A laptop with Python 3.10 or newer installed and at least 16 GB RAM.

Enough free disk space for the Python packages, datasets and model weights. We will confirm the requirement with the setup instructions before the masterclass. We will also share instructions for downloading the model weights beforehand, which saves time on the day.

Optional: access to an AI coding assistant of your choice for the data exploration exercises.

Optional: your own dataset as a CSV with timestamps, a series identifier, the target and any covariates. Only bring data you are allowed to use in the workshop, and do not upload confidential data to cloud AI tools.

# Your trainers

## Irena Bojarovska

Irena Bojarovska is an Applied Scientist at Zalando, working in forecasting since 2021. Her experience spans market-level time-series forecasting and, more recently, article-level demand forecasting. She led a team evaluating time series foundation models at Zalando’s company-wide hackathon, where the team won the Business Potential Award. She also coordinated a team during a follow-up research sprint in the Pricing department to investigate practical applications of these models, and later put one into production. She co-runs an internal time-series research-paper group and shares her work and experience at conferences and meetups, including PyCon DE 2026. She holds a PhD in Applied Harmonic Analysis from TU Berlin and teaches mathematics to children at Lyzeum 2 e.V.

[YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/@irenabojarovska)

## Tijana Atanasovska

Tijana Atanasovska is pursuing an MSc focused on time-series forecasting, researching continual learning for forecasting and anomaly detection in renewable energy systems, so that models can adapt as data patterns change. She is a data and machine learning engineer at MagixAI with five years of experience building scalable data platforms, end-to-end data pipelines and AI-driven solutions. She also contributed to MKLLM-7B, the first open-source large language model for the Macedonian language. Driven by her love for Python and data, she is an active member of the PyData Skopje community, and has contributed to organizing several PyData events.

[LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/tijana-atanasovska-0b7b99201)

